   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The definition of 'shot mix' needs to be precise and reliable. If it's too granular, it might suffer from sparsity; if too broad, it might obscure real effects. The 'personnel effects' also need a clear, actionable definition."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "Given that previous studies (FAM:SF34) found small, sub-1pp shifts in shot mix related outcomes, detecting a meaningful 'move' in shot mix will require a large sample size and robust statistical power, especially if the effects are subtle."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.2,
  "rejected": 0.3,
  "untestable": 0.1,
  "artifact": 0.25,
  "uncertain": 0.15
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of changing the offensive five's personnel composition (e.g., adding/removing a specific player type, or changing a specific player) on the team's shot mix (e.g., proportion of rim attempts, 3-pointers, mid-range shots, or specific play types like drives or post-ups) per possession, controlling for player skill, opponent, and game context.",
  "unit": "Offensive possession (team-level).",
  "sample": "All offensive possessions from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, and 2024-25 regular seasons and playoffs. Filter for possessions where the offensive lineup remains constant. Consider only changes in the offensive five that occur during live play (substitutions) or between possessions, not between halves/quarters unless the analysis explicitly accounts for such breaks.",
  "power": "A power analysis will be conducted based on anticipated effect sizes (e.g., 0.5-1.0 percentage point shift in a shot mix component) and observed variance in historical data. Given the large N (millions of possessions), the primary challenge will be isolating effects rather than raw sample size for overall detection. Effects for specific lineup changes might still be underpowered.",
  "controls": "Statistical models (e.g., panel regressions, fixed effects models) will be used to control for confounding variables: \n1. Player Fixed Effects: To account for the overall skill and tendencies of individual players in the lineup. \n2. Team Fixed Effects: To account for team-specific offensive schemes and coaching tendencies. \n3. Opponent Fixed Effects: To control for the defensive schemes and personnel of the opposing team. \n4. Game State Covariates: Score differential, time remaining in quarter/game. \n5. Contextual Controls: Home/away, rest days, opponent quality (e.g., by ORtg/DRtg). \n6. Matchup-specific controls: If 'personnel effects' are defined granularly, defensive matchup data will be incorporated.",
  "falsification": "Placebo tests: \n1. Analyze the 'effect' of offensive lineup changes on outcomes known not to be directly affected by offensive personnel (e.g., opponent free throw rate, or a randomly permuted 'shot mix' metric). \n2. Test for effects of 'personnel changes' on shot mix that occur when no actual personnel change happened (e.g., using lagged lineup composition as a 'fake' change). \n3. Compare effects of personnel changes that are clearly driven by injury or foul trouble vs. strategic changes.",
  "multiplicity": "If multiple shot mix components (e.g., rim, mid-range, 3PA proportions) or multiple definitions of 'personnel effects' are tested, p-value adjustments (e.g., Bonferroni, Benjamini-Hochberg) will be applied or a primary outcome measure will be pre-specified.",
  "decision_rule": "The hypothesis will be supported if a statistically significant (p < 0.05 after multiplicity correction) and practically meaningful (effect size defined pre-analysis) change in the pre-specified shot mix component is observed, and robust to falsification tests and alternative model specifications. The design's ability to 'detect personnel effects' will be demonstrated if such an effect is found and robustly attributed to personnel changes rather than confounders.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 regular season and playoffs data as available on 2026-11-02."
 }

